{
  "dom_id": "question-167646",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[6]{x}$ equals\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\,\\text{ }C$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $\\sqrt[6]{x}={x}^{1/6}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{6}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/6+1}}{(\\frac{1}{6}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/6}}{(\\frac{7}{6})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/6})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{6}{x}^{7/6-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/6}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
